Pipe shells along an auxiliary spine with curvilinear equivalence: the
guide trihedron's second derivative of its binormal (mitcad#539).

GeomFill_GuideTrihedronAC::D2 takes the binormal's second derivative as
that of a unit vector b = u / |u| (u the tangent crossed with the normal),
b'' = d2 - 2 (b.d) d + (3 (b.d)^2 - |d|^2 - b.d2) b with d = u' / |u| and
d2 = u'' / |u|, as it does for the normal. For |d|^2 it used the square of
b itself (TN after it is made a unit vector: 1), which adds -(1 - |d|^2) b
to the result. The binormal's value and first derivative are right (D0,
D1), so the sections of the pipe are where they belong, but the sweep's
approximation, which takes the second derivatives, bends its surfaces
between them: an 8 x 2 bar swept 50 along a line, turned a quarter turn by
an auxiliary spine whose points correspond to the line's at the same
fraction of the lengths, held 784.9 (775.9 at a tolerance of 1e-7) of its
800 (every section is the bar: 800 by Cavalieri), its side faces up to
0.27 off the turned bar between the sections. With |d|^2 the result is
800, as by the plane rule (GuideTrihedronPlan) that turns the bar alike.

